{
  "reason": "The current sampler supplies 16 negatives, while SASRec.forward and fit_member already implement active-position scoring and positive-plus-negatives cross-entropy; these are the interfaces needed to increase coverage to 64 without changing inference. Historical success supports uniform multi-negative supervision, while failed hard-negative mining and recency weighting favor isolating negative count. Host macro HR@10 of 0.5625 and Electronics HR@10 of 0.370 motivate this experiment but do not establish insufficient negative coverage as the cause.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "In SequentialHistories.__getitem__, change negative allocation from (self.maxlen, 16) to (self.maxlen, 64) and neg sampling from (length, 16) to (length, 64). Retain independent uniform sampling with replacement over IDs 1 through self.nitems and rejection against self.seen[index], which contains the complete training history; duplicate negatives remain allowed and validation interactions must not enter this exclusion set. This increases competitor coverage while preserving the sampling distribution supported by historical experiments, without introducing failed hard-negative mining. Preserve constructor and method signatures, variable names, the returned seq, pos, negative tuple, zero-padded rows, chronological truncation, eligible histories, make_loader, worker_seed, and the independent MultVAE Histories pipeline.",
    "model_design": "Keep SASRec.forward(self, seq, pos, neg) dimension-generic so the expanded sampler produces positive logits shaped (N_active,) and negative logits shaped (N_active, 64), retaining its two-value return interface. Preserve active = pos.ne(0), select sequence features and negative IDs before embedding lookup, and retain the existing einsum scoring so padding incurs no negative-embedding computation. This directly supports the larger cross-entropy denominator while avoiding architectural confounds; preserve SASRec initialization, attention blocks, gated causal prefix-mean preference branch, log2feats, predict, candidate scoring, and all MultVAE parameters and behavior. Do not add hard-negative selection, item biases, temperature changes, sampling corrections, recency weighting, or auxiliary losses.",
    "training_evaluation": "In fit_member, retain concatenation of positive[:, None] and negative followed by float32 F.cross_entropy with class-zero targets and uniform averaging over active positions; the expanded input must now yield 65-way logits without changing the loss formula. Add small deterministic checks, restoring RNG state afterward, for 64-negative shapes, zero padding, complete-training-history exclusion, cross-entropy reference agreement, finite loss, and gradient flow; retain existing checkpoint and exported-predictor agreement checks. Explicitly preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, evaluation seed 42, supplied splits, all four categories and dataset_name routes, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, Adam settings, learning rate, batch size, steps, SASREC_EPOCHS default 200, validation cadence, checkpoint selection, early stopping, runtime allocations, single-GPU execution, and the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Preserve MultVAE reconstruction and KL annealing, reciprocal-rank fusion with constant 60, the five-weight validation selection procedure, metadata, load_ensemble, PREDICTOR, and its writer because inference architecture and scoring remain unchanged. Compare independent host macro HR@10 against 0.5625 and report all category scores, especially Electronics against 0.370 and baseline 0.420, using the unchanged 200-user, 99-unique-negative protocol; treat any improvement as unmeasured until host validation and leave held-out evaluation exclusively to the host."
  }
}
